## Domain relations

- `Node → NodeType`, many-to-one, a reference. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.Node` relation `type`, cardinality one, via `type_id`). This is why a match compares exactly one `type_id`.
- `GraphRoot → Node`, one-to-many, the root owns its nodes. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.GraphRoot` relation `nodes`, `owns`, via `root_id`). Candidates come from the canonical root only.
- `Node → alias`, one-to-many, the node owns its aliases, and nothing stops two nodes holding the same alias. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.Node` field `aliases: List<String>`; this is a field, not a `relations:` entry, and no `ess/1` declaration makes an alias unique). That is why `Ambiguous` exists.
- `NodeType → parent NodeType`, many-to-many. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/ontology.yaml`, `ekr.ontology.NodeType` fields `parents: List<TypeId>` and `abstract_type`). Used here only to detect and refuse a type with descendants.

## Tests the story ships

- Two nodes with the same `canonical_name` and different types: a reference to either type resolves to that type node only.
- A property test: permuting the snapshot node order or the reference alias order gives byte-identical outcomes.
- An alias differing by case or whitespace does not match.
- The crate declares no string-similarity dependency, held by a test that reads its manifest.

## Scope

- `crates/ekr-integrate/` (new crate: `Cargo.toml`, `src/lib.rs`, `src/resolve.rs`, `tests/resolve.rs`)
- `Cargo.toml` (workspace): the one `members` line

## Acceptance

Over one canonical snapshot, a typed reference resolves to the single node of exactly its type that holds one of its aliases, to `ProposeNew` when no node does, and to `Ambiguous` listing every candidate when more than one does, and two nodes sharing a name but differing in type never resolve to each other.
